# Every `entries` builds one record a key, and for a while it built two
# allocations a key: an arena block holding the pair, and the record that
# copied the pair out of it and never looked at the block again. k_rec
# stores its fields immediately after the header, so the block was written
# once, read once and dropped.
#
# The records then stopped taking a bump apiece as well. They were already
# landing next to each other, since k_alloc only moves a pointer, so entries
# takes one block for all of them and carves it.
#
# A thousand walks of an eight-key map is eight thousand records in one
# thousand blocks, and `allocs` below counts the blocks. Restore the per-record
# bump and it reads 10010; restore the scratch pair on top of that and it reads
# 18010. `alloc_bytes` and `sh_rec` are the same in all three, which is the
# point: the records occupy the same arena at the same stride either way, and
# only the arithmetic that reserved them moved.
#
# The encode benchmarks see this shape three million times a run, and a counter
# that moves by one on a one-key map cannot tell anyone that.
fn walk 0 acc _
  acc

fn walk n acc m
  walk (n - 1) (acc + length (entries m)) m

eight = { "a":1 "b":2 "c":3 "d":4 "e":5 "f":6 "g":7 "h":8 }

pub play = print (walk 1000 0 eight)
